 {:title "Kotoba, the Turing tarpit, and Rice's theorem"
  :description "How Kotoba combines expressive programming with effect checks, capability enforcement and target-specific resource limits."
  :intro "A language needs useful abstractions, and a safety checker needs an honest scope. Kotoba addresses these through a high-level surface and explicit computation boundaries. Those boundaries do not establish arbitrary functional correctness or successful termination."
  :notice "Design note reviewed on 23 October 2026. This is a source-based explanation, not a new runtime qualification or proof of Turing completeness. English is the documentation source of record; a Japanese version is provided."
  :sections
  [{:title "The tarpit is a usability problem"
    :paragraphs ["Perlis's Turing tarpit describes the gap between being able to express computation and being able to write useful programs conveniently. Computational universality alone gives no usability guarantee."
                 "Kotoba keeps a small checked core while offering collections, destructuring, records and higher-order functions within supported profiles. Its grammar grows expressiveness through elaboration into existing primitives or declared capability kits. Actual applications, workarounds and diagnostics determine how well that design works."]}
   {:title "Rice's theorem sets the scope of analysis"
    :paragraphs ["For arbitrary programs in a universal computation model, no terminating algorithm can decide every case of a nontrivial property of the partial function a program computes. This leaves room for restricted program classes, individual proofs, conservative analysis and runtime enforcement."
                 "Kotoba propagates capability effects over a closed call graph to a fixed point. It can describe possible effects conservatively without deciding exactly which branch every input will reach. A checker can reject a program whose actual behavior would have been acceptable."]}
   {:title "Authority is checked at the operation boundary"
    :paragraphs ["The runtime combines admitted compiler evidence, the concrete intent, delegated scope, local policy and current provider availability before supplying a capability. A component without effective network authority cannot use the correctly enforced provider boundary to send data."
                 "That guarantee depends on the compiler, host and provider implementing the contract. An operation within a broad grant can still be a harmful choice; authorization alone does not establish user intent or business correctness."]}
   {:title "Fuel does not prove every program terminates"
    :paragraphs ["Kotoba admits recursion and loop/recur without requiring a general termination proof. Reference and Wasm profiles describe a default call-fuel budget of 512, but synthesized loop helpers can re-enter without consuming it. Provider time and instruction interruption require separate controls."
                 "The POSIX native kexe profile has deterministic fuel metering off by default and separately specifies an OS emergency stop. Other native profiles have different defaults. Check the selected target and actual host enforcement. Being interrupted at a limit is different from computing the intended answer successfully."]}
   {:title "Keep the computation model explicit"
    :paragraphs ["A fixed execution with finite input/state domains and an effective bound on every transition can be explored exhaustively in principle, often at impractical cost. That does not decide the corresponding unbounded problem. Neither recursion nor self-hosting alone proves Turing completeness."
                 "The useful claim is scoped: the checked structure and enforced operation boundary constrain what an admitted run can do. General semantic correctness and successful termination need their own evidence."]}
   {:title "A CID identifies a definition or result"
    :paragraphs ["DefCID, AdmissionCID and ValueCID identify different objects: the selected definition, admitted execution context and resulting value. They support verification and reproduction within the identity contract. They do not decide arbitrary semantic equivalence, and an identified or signed definition can contain a logic error."]}]
  :source-title "Contracts, implementation evidence and theory"
